{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nEvery rebar ticket write on a real store logs\n`WARNING rebar.llm.enrich_drain: enrich drain gate check took 500-740 ms (> 20 ms budget)`.\nObserved on EVERY write in a 2026-08-19 session against this repo's own tracker.\n\nMeasured (worktree of origin/main at 09947b1, warm page cache, 4710 ticket dirs in\n`.tickets-tracker`):\n\n    Q.pending_enrichment(...) x3 -> 797.9 ms, 743.5 ms, 724.6 ms   (pending=0 each time)\n    Q.reduce_ticket x300         -> 38.5 ms  (~0.128 ms/ticket)\n\n0.128 ms/ticket x 4710 = ~600 ms, which accounts for the whole figure. So this is NOT a cold\n-path/first-run cost (it repeats warm, run after run) and NOT a recent regression: the gate is\nLINEAR in the number of ticket directories, and `maybe_drain`'s 20 ms budget\n(`overlap_drain_gate_budget_ms`) is only reachable at roughly <=150 tickets. The store crossed\nthat threshold long ago.\n\n`maybe_drain`'s docstring calls this a \"cheap 'is anything soaked?' check that no-ops in the\ncommon case\". It is not cheap: `overlap.queue.pending_enrichment` lists the tracker and calls\n`reduce_ticket` for EVERY ticket dir, each of which scans that ticket's events three times\n(`_latest` for ENQUEUE / CLAIM / DONE). The common case — nothing soaked — costs a full store\nwalk.\n\nTwo distinct harms:\n1. **Latency on the write path.** `maybe_drain` runs opportunistically on ordinary\n   invocations whenever `verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets` is on, so every ticket write pays\n   ~0.6-0.8 s of pure overhead before returning.\n2. **A dead observability signal.** The budget breach is designed to be a warning an operator\n   acts on. Firing on 100% of writes makes it noise, so a genuine future breach is invisible.\n\n## Approach (CHOSEN — recorded on claim, 2026-08-20)\nThe first candidate below, confirmed by measurement (see the root-cause comment): make the\ngate O(1) rather than O(tickets), via a marker under `.rebar`.\n\nThe queue is a SOAK queue, so a quiet store is not merely quiet now — it is provably quiet\nuntil the earliest instant any live entry could become pending. One scan computes that\ninstant; every probe before it is then answerable from a single small file read.\n\n- A marker `.rebar/enrich-gate.json` holding `{next_eligible_ns, written_ns}`, meaning \"no\n  queue entry can become pending before `next_eligible_ns`\" (`null` = no live entry at all).\n  It lives in the repo-local `.rebar` (the tracker's SIBLING, where the drain lock already\n  lives) and never inside the git-backed tracker, which auto-commits and auto-pushes.\n- `pending_enrichment` keeps its exact signature, return type, sort order and answers. It\n  returns `[]` immediately when a live marker proves the store quiet; otherwise it runs the\n  existing single scan and, in that SAME pass, computes the soonest eligibility (a claimed\n  entry with a live lease defers to `max(not_before_ns, lease_expires_ns)`; a done or\n  never-enqueued entry contributes nothing) and records the marker.\n- Invalidation goes in `_append`, the single funnel for ENQUEUE / CLAIM / DONE, so no queue\n  state change can hide behind the marker.\n- Self-healing, three ways: the marker can only ever say \"nothing is pending\" (it can never\n  manufacture work, so a wrong marker costs a delayed drain, never a wrong one); any queue\n  append clears it; and it expires after a TTL well inside the 60-minute default soak, so a\n  crash between the append and the clear defers a drain by at most the TTL rather than losing\n  it. Every read and write is best-effort — a missing, empty, truncated, non-JSON or\n  wrong-typed marker degrades to the full scan, which is today's behaviour.\n- Marker writes are atomic (temp file in the same dir + `os.replace`), so a concurrent reader\n  sees the old marker or the new one, never a torn file.\n\nThe second candidate (recalibrating `overlap_drain_gate_budget_ms` with store size) is\nREJECTED: it would silence the signal without removing the ~24,000 metadata syscalls per\nwrite that are the actual harm.\n\nKeeps the drain's existing no-op-on-Windows / never-raise contract: nothing on this path\nraises, and `enrich_drain.py` is untouched.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` (the gate + its budget warning)\n- `src/rebar/llm/overlap/queue.py` (`pending_enrichment`, and any marker written by\n  `enqueue` / `mark_done`)\n- corresponding tests under `tests/unit/`\n\n## Testing\n- A test that plants N enqueued tickets and asserts the gate's work does not grow linearly\n  with N (e.g. counts `reduce_ticket` calls, rather than asserting a wall-clock number, so it\n  is not flaky on a loaded CI runner).\n- A test that a lost/corrupt marker still finds a genuinely soaked ticket (self-healing).\n- A test that the budget warning does not fire once per write on a large store.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The gate's cost on a store with no soaked entries is independent of the ticket count,\n      asserted by a call-count test rather than a wall-clock assertion.\n- [ ] A soaked ticket is still found after the fast path's marker is deleted or corrupted.\n- [ ] The budget-breach WARNING no longer fires on an ordinary write against a store of\n      several thousand tickets.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and CI's pytest invocation are green.\n\n## Provenance\nDiscovered while implementing task deathful-lettered-maltesedog (8d78-ef59-83cd-4d76); out of\nthat ticket's scope, filed rather than fixed there."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787260461051019001,"uuid":"e5e1678b-c898-41b9-9fe6-0a12fafeae85"}